Turin Canon, marks him as one of several short-lived rulers who struggled to maintain the centralized authority of the Old Kingdom. His existence underscores the profound political and administrative decay that followed the reign of Pepi II Neferkare, signaling a significant departure from the millennia-long stability of unified Egypt.

During his time on the throne, Neferkare Pepiseneb likely held little actual power beyond the immediate vicinity of Memphis, the traditional capital. Monumental evidence from his reign is virtually nonexistent, a stark contrast to the grand pyramids and elaborate mortuary complexes of his Old Kingdom predecessors. Egyptologists like Kim Ryholt and Jürgen Beckerath place him as the twelfth ruler of the Eighth Dynasty, a period characterized by a rapid succession of kings, many bearing the throne name Neferkare, suggesting either a conscious attempt to evoke the stability of Pepi II's long reign or perhaps a rapid turnover within the royal family itself, unable to establish firm control.

Neferkare Pepiseneb's legacy is not one of great achievement or lasting monuments, but rather as a historical marker of Egypt's descent into decentralization. His reign, alongside those of his immediate predecessors and successors in the Eighth Dynasty, represents the complete erosion of the pharaoh's absolute power and the rise of provincial nomarchs who increasingly governed their territories as independent potentates. This fragmentation laid the groundwork for the subsequent two centuries of the First Intermediate Period, an era of regional conflicts, shifting alliances, and a profound reorientation of Egyptian society and belief systems."}, {"timestamp": "2026-07-27T07:24:52Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#30245631459", "field": "detailsJson.causes", "oldValue": [{"title": "Classical era: flourishing of philosophical inquiry, imperial expansion, and codification of la", "type": "EventWindow", "year": "2200 BCE"}, {"title": "Political crises and power transitions requiring new leadership", "type": "Idea", "year": "2200 BCE"}], "newValue": ["The collapse of the Old Kingdom's highly centralized administration created a power vacuum throughout Egypt.", "The increasing autonomy and wealth of provincial nomarchs challenged the traditional authority of the pharaoh.", "Economic decline and localized control over resources further weakened the central government's ability to maintain order."]}, {"timestamp": "2026-07-27T07:24:52Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#30245631459", "field": "detailsJson.effects", "oldValue": [{"title": "Shaped political institutions and governance practices", "type": "Idea", "year": "2200 BCE"}, {"title": "Contributed to enduring intellectual and institutional frameworks shaping subsequent civilizati", "type": "Movement", "year": "2200 BCE"}], "newValue": ["Neferkare Pepiseneb's reign contributed to the perception of ongoing political instability characteristic of the early First Intermediate Period.", "The inability of phara